      ⍝ Bugs28 #39 regression (Bugs27 #24 residual): ¯1⋆B for an
      ⍝ integral B ≥ 2⋆63 returned complex noise instead of the exact
      ⍝ real result. The #24 fix's exact-integer path only applies
      ⍝ when B fits an int64_t (|B| < 9.2E18); a larger B fell through
      ⍝ to complex_power() = exp(B×ln A), whose imaginary part B×π is
      ⍝ pure rounding noise at that magnitude. Every double of
      ⍝ magnitude ≥ 2⋆53 is necessarily an EVEN integer (a double's
      ⍝ mantissa has only 52 bits, so the ulp is already ≥ 2 by then),
      ⍝ so A⋆B for A<0 and such a B is real and positive, exactly
      ⍝ pow(|A|,B) -- no parity trick or complex path needed. Fixed in
      ⍝ FloatCell::bif_power_ff().
      ⍝
      ¯1*1E20
1

      ¯1*1E300
1

      ⍝ control: below 2⋆63, the exact int64 path was already correct
      ⍝
      ¯1*1E15+1
¯1

      ⍝ Bugs28 #40 regression (Bugs27 #24/#59(a) residual): a
      ⍝ complex-typed power with an integral exponent went through
      ⍝ exp/log noise instead of the exact repeated-squaring path,
      ⍝ whenever either operand was complex-TYPED even if really a
      ⍝ real number (e.g. 52J0, or a real A with a FloatCell-typed B
      ⍝ promoted to complex for the call, like ¯1J0⋆1E14). Fixed in
      ⍝ ComplexCell::bif_power_cc(): when both A and B are really real
      ⍝ (zero imaginary part), delegate entirely to
      ⍝ FloatCell::bif_power_ff() -- which already computes this
      ⍝ exactly for every magnitude of B, including the huge-exponent
      ⍝ case of Bugs28 #39; when only A is genuinely complex, route a
      ⍝ real integral B through bif_power_ci()'s exact
      ⍝ binary-exponentiation instead.
      ⍝
      ⎕PP←17
